cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Cicada 3301 Research Archive and Liber Primus Cryptanalysis"
message: >-
  If you use this repository's results, please cite the specific round or lane
  and its verdict file rather than the repository as a whole, so a reader can
  check the individual claim. Example:
  "Round 12 front D3, liber-primus/analysis/round12/D3/RESULTS.md".
type: software
authors:
  - alias: Dukotah
    website: "https://github.com/Dukotah"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Dukotah/cicada3301"
abstract: >-
  A documented, falsifiable investigation of the Cicada 3301 puzzles and a
  validated pure-Python cryptanalysis rig aimed at the unsolved Liber Primus
  pages (LP2 0-54, 12,956 runes).

  The central result is a negative with a precisely stated scope: the unsolved
  pages are OTP-class, meaning the ciphertext cannot be distinguished between a
  true external one-time pad (information-theoretically closed) and a keystream
  derived from a short seed (a finite, brute-forceable keyspace). Which of those
  it is remains unsettled and is the subject of ongoing work.

  The repository's methodological contribution is a discipline for producing
  negative results that are worth something: every attack is pre-registered with
  its threshold fixed in advance, every instrument must first recover a planted
  signal before its silence is trusted, and every null is size-matched and
  corrected for the number of trials actually run. It ships that discipline as a
  reusable benchmark, a machine-readable falsification ledger, and an oracle that
  adjudicates candidate solutions against criteria committed to in advance.

  It also records its own corrections, including an overstated headline verdict,
  three closures that were not as closed as claimed, a silently truncated input
  file, and two measurement errors in its own instruments.
keywords:
  - cryptanalysis
  - cicada-3301
  - liber-primus
  - classical-ciphers
  - one-time-pad
  - reproducible-research
  - pre-registration
  - negative-results
